Given a linearly independent set of vectors in $CC^n$, the Gram-Schmidt process replaces them step by step, subtracting multiples of earlier directions, so that each intermediate set spans the same subspace while the processed vectors become mutually orthonormal.

Finding an orthonormal basis $B_lambda$ of each eigenspace $E_lambda$ of a Hermitian matrix $matbold(A)$ and taking $B = B_(lambda_1) union dots.v union B_(lambda_r)$ over the distinct eigenvalues therefore yields an orthonormal set of $CC^n$ consisting of eigenvectors of $matbold(A)$.
